  ('c47b7030-53f9-4f90-99d7-b1da806790ab', '8af718c6-ca11-482b-9d6b-832f2b767928', 'f1946e0e-32ca-4cf0-86d5-134ac543faa5', '39c6c5a7-f3e7-4070-a1e4-1220b68775f5', 'c714d675-9f79-4969-aa3e-70bc893fc2e7', '4b2248fa-831d-47df-8ee2-e7a1722b39f6', $qbx$Imagine a group of students from diverse backgrounds participating in the 'Students on Ice' program. They are taken to the South Pole, where they witness firsthand the dramatic retreat of glaciers and the collapse of ice shelves. During their educational sessions, they delve into the microscopic world of phytoplankton, understanding their role as the 'grasses of the sea' and their vital process of photosynthesis. Their instructor explained that scientists warn about the impact of ozone layer depletion on phytoplankton, which could cascade to affect marine life, birds, and the global carbon cycle. The instructor concluded by sharing the 'parable of the phytoplankton': 'take care of the small things and the big things will fall into place.'$qbx$, 'long_answer'::question_type, NULL, NULL, 1, 5, ARRAY[]::text[], 'ai_xlsx_import_xlsx_2026-06-08T09-55-16-399Z', 'ba219b8a-c17f-46af-b642-be52f18c80ff', true, NULL, 4, NULL),
  ('f3591f79-171c-4bef-8790-4c555073ed25', '8af718c6-ca11-482b-9d6b-832f2b767928', 'f1946e0e-32ca-4cf0-86d5-134ac543faa5', '39c6c5a7-f3e7-4070-a1e4-1220b68775f5', 'c714d675-9f79-4969-aa3e-70bc893fc2e7', '4b2248fa-831d-47df-8ee2-e7a1722b39f6', $qbx$Consider a scenario where a nation's policy-makers are debating environmental regulations. Some argue that minor environmental changes in distant regions, like Antarctica, have negligible impact on their national interests, citing the comfort of their geographical location. However, a former participant of the 'Students on Ice' program presents evidence from their expedition: visible glacier retreat and ice shelf collapse. They further explain the delicate balance of Antarctica's simple ecosystem, highlighting the critical role of phytoplankton and the scientific warnings about ozone depletion's effects on them, which in turn impacts the global carbon cycle. The former participant concludes by referencing the 'parable of the phytoplankton' to underscore the interconnectedness of all environmental elements.$qbx$, 'long_answer'::question_type, NULL, NULL, 1, 5, ARRAY[]::text[], 'ai_xlsx_import_xlsx_2026-06-08T09-55-16-399Z', 'ba219b8a-c17f-46af-b642-be52f18c80ff', true, NULL, 4, NULL),
  ('2cfde5d3-9608-4b05-8e8d-ded38ead82a7', '8af718c6-ca11-482b-9d6b-832f2b767928', 'f1946e0e-32ca-4cf0-86d5-134ac543faa5', '39c6c5a7-f3e7-4070-a1e4-1220b68775f5', 'c714d675-9f79-4969-aa3e-70bc893fc2e7', '4b2248fa-831d-47df-8ee2-e7a1722b39f6', $qbx$A marine biologist is conducting research in the Southern Ocean, focusing on the health of the phytoplankton population. Her preliminary findings indicate a decline in certain phytoplankton species, which she attributes to localized ozone layer thinning. She understands that these 'grasses of the sea' are fundamental to the entire food chain, converting sunlight into organic compounds through photosynthesis. The biologist is concerned that if this trend continues, it will not only decimate local marine animal and bird populations but also disrupt the global carbon cycle. She reflects on the 'parable of the phytoplankton,' realizing the profound implications of these microscopic changes for the entire planet.$qbx$, 'long_answer'::question_type, NULL, NULL, 1, 5, ARRAY[]::text[], 'ai_xlsx_import_xlsx_2026-06-08T09-55-16-399Z', 'ba219b8a-c17f-46af-b642-be52f18c80ff', true, NULL, 4, NULL),
  ('41bd00c4-515a-444e-a834-69e745ea67d2', '8af718c6-ca11-482b-9d6b-832f2b767928', 'f1946e0e-32ca-4cf0-86d5-134ac543faa5', '39c6c5a7-f3e7-4070-a1e4-1220b68775f5', 'c714d675-9f79-4969-aa3e-70bc893fc2e7', '4b2248fa-831d-47df-8ee2-e7a1722b39f6', $qbx$An international environmental summit is underway, discussing strategies to combat global warming. One delegate argues for focusing resources solely on large-scale industrial emissions, dismissing the significance of seemingly minor ecological shifts in remote areas. Another delegate, who once participated in a 'Students on Ice' expedition, counters this argument. She recounts her experience of witnessing glaciers retreating and ice shelves collapsing in Antarctica. She then explains the delicate nature of Antarctica's simple ecosystem and the crucial role of microscopic phytoplankton, which, if affected by ozone depletion, could disrupt the entire Southern Ocean food chain and the global carbon cycle. She concludes by invoking the 'parable of the phytoplankton' to emphasize that 'take care of the small things and the big things will fall into place.'$qbx$, 'long_answer'::question_type, NULL, NULL, 1, 5, ARRAY[]::text[], 'ai_xlsx_import_xlsx_2026-06-08T09-55-16-399Z', 'ba219b8a-c17f-46af-b642-be52f18c80ff', true, NULL, 4, NULL),
  ('9a31cbdb-ffb3-4ae9-9b83-15ee596919eb', '8af718c6-ca11-482b-9d6b-832f2b767928', 'f1946e0e-32ca-4cf0-86d5-134ac543faa5', '39c6c5a7-f3e7-4070-a1e4-1220b68775f5', 'c714d675-9f79-4969-aa3e-70bc893fc2e7', '4b2248fa-831d-47df-8ee2-e7a1722b39f6', $qbx$22 Vistas Walk on the ocean My Antarctic experience was full of such epiphanies, but the best occurred just short of the Antarctic Circle at 65.55 degrees south. The Shokalskiy had managed to wedge herself into a thick white stretch of ice between the peninsula and Tadpole Island which was preventing us from going any further. The Captain decided we were going to turn around and head back north, but before we did, we were all instructed to climb down the gangplank and walk on the ocean. So there we were, all 52 of us, kitted out in Gore-Tex and glares, walking on a stark whiteness that seemed to spread out forever. Underneath our feet was a metre-thick ice pack, and underneath that, 180 metres of living, breathing, salt water. In the periphery Crabeater seals were stretching and sunning themselves on ice floes much like stray dogs will do under the shade of a banyan tree. It was nothing short of a revelation: everything does indeed connect. Nine time zones, six checkpoints, three bodies of water and many ecospheres later, I was still wondering about the beauty of balance in play on our planet. How would it be if Antarctica were to become the warm place that it once used to be? Will we be around to see it, or would we have gone the way of the dinosaurs, mammoths and woolly rhinos? Who’s to say? But after spending two weeks with a bunch of teenagers who still have the idealism to save the world, all I can say is that a lot can happen in a million years, but what a difference a day makes! Reading with Insight 1. ‘The world’s geological history is trapped in Antarctica.’ How is the study of this region useful to us? 2. What are Geoff Green’s reasons for including high school students in the Students on Ice expedition? Reprint 2026-27$qbx$, 'long_answer'::question_type, NULL, NULL, 1, 5, ARRAY[]::text[], 'ai_xlsx_import_xlsx_2026-06-08T09-55-16-399Z', 'ba219b8a-c17f-46af-b642-be52f18c80ff', true, NULL, 3, NULL),
